 **Parameters:**
-*   **Loss Type:** L1 norm is specified in the source [S1].
-*   **Sampling:** Standard NeRF sampling strategy is implied, though high-frequency positional encodings are noted to cause overfitting in sparse regimes [S1].
+*   **Weighting:** The loss is typically applied as a primary term or combined with photometric loss. In SPARF, it is combined with photometric loss [S1].
+*   **Point Matching:** The specific method for finding the "nearest" ground-truth point is not detailed in the primary text, but implies a correspondence exists per pixel [S1].
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Sparse View Overfitting:** Standard NeRF with high-frequency positional encodings immediately overfits to provided images, leading to degenerate geometry, even with perfect poses [S1]. Simplified MLPs or training without positional encoding may be required to mitigate this [S1].
-*   **Pose Noise:** The method is designed to handle noisy poses (e.g., 15% additive Gaussian noise) [S1].
-*   **Background:** Results are often computed by masking the background to focus on the scene geometry [S1].
+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Sparse Views:** The method is specifically designed for sparse-view regimes (e.g., 3 views) where standard depth loss fails [S1].
+*   **Noisy Poses:** It is effective when initial poses are noisy (e.g., perturbed by 15% Gaussian noise) [S1].
+*   **Overfitting:** Standard NeRF with high-frequency positional encoding overfits immediately; this loss helps mitigate degenerate geometry [S1].
 
